      "id": 1,
      "options": {
        "content": "# Polaris RabbitMQ\n\nNative RabbitMQ metrics come from the `rabbitmq_prometheus` plugin on\n`:15692` (see `infra/prometheus/prometheus.yml`, job `polaris-rabbitmq`).\nCommon series:\n\n- `rabbitmq_queue_messages_published_total{queue}` \u2014 publishes per stream/queue\n- `rabbitmq_queue_messages_ready{queue}` / `rabbitmq_queue_messages_unacked{queue}` \u2014 depth\n- `rabbitmq_queue_consumers{queue}` \u2014 is anything reading this stream?\n- `rabbitmq_channel_messages_unroutable_dropped_total` \u2014 publishes with no binding\n- `up{job=\"polaris-rabbitmq\"}` \u2014 scrape health\n\n**What changed with the migration.** Kafka's consumer-group lag metric has\nno RabbitMQ equivalent: streams do not track per-consumer position\nserver-side, which is exactly why Polaris owns checkpoints in\n`transport_checkpoints`. The authoritative lag signal is therefore\n`polaris_processor_lag_ms_last` (event-time based, emitted by every\nprocessor) \u2014 the same series the alert rules page on, unchanged across\nthe migration.\n\n**Unroutable publishes are a topology alarm.** RabbitMQ auto-creates\nnothing. A non-zero `rabbitmq_channel_messages_unroutable_dropped_total`\nmeans someone is publishing to an exchange with no binding \u2014 run\n`pnpm rabbitmq:provision` and check\n`docs/operations/runbook-rabbitmq-topology.md`.\n\n**Polaris-side proxy.** The broker exposes no per-producer publish-failure\ncounter. The closest signal is the ingester's batch-rejection-with-\n`reason=publish_failed` counter \u2014 included below as the publish-failure\nrate.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "11.4.0",
      "title": "About this dashboard",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
